# Review: DiscordPlugin

The three connector operations and the channel backend all exist. Discovery, materialization and the
pull-only sync are wired into two `ConnectorSpec.Connector` entries (bot token and user OAuth), and the
bot-token form validates against `GET /users/@me`. The channel backend is the best-covered part:
`channel-backend.test.ts` checks chunked posts, DMs, mentions and the EDGE start/stop/status routes. The
sync path has no test. `sync.test.ts` is a credential-gated live test of a testing helper, not of the
operation, and `discord-source.test.ts` tests the crawler's own message mapper, not the copy in `sync.ts`.
The spec has also drifted from the code. Channels are created eagerly by `MaterializeDiscordTarget` when a
binding is made, not on first sync. Sync never renames a channel, has no legacy foreign-key fallback and
uses no per-target transaction. `Binding.syncAll` runs two bindings at once, not three, and fails the
whole operation once any binding fails, after every binding has had its turn. The plugin also ships much
the spec does not cover: agent and admin operations, the back-index crawler and the bot setup UI.

Of 42 items: 2 done, 23 untested, 1 partial, 2 diverged, 14 missing.

